# The NVENC entry points the streamer calls.
#
# Fed to the same generator contract-bus uses for iceoryx2.sigs: a dlsym-backed
# dispatch table, so there is no -lnvencodeapi at link time and no NVIDIA SDK on
# the include path. The binary builds on a machine that has never seen an NVIDIA
# driver and fails at start rather than at link when one is absent.
#
# NVENC exports exactly two symbols. Verified on this desk against
# C:\Windows\System32\nvEncodeAPI64.dll, driver 32.0.16.1692:
#
#     1  NvEncodeAPICreateInstance
#     2  NvEncodeAPIGetMaxSupportedVersion
#
# THIS IS WHERE THE PATTERN STOPS SHORT, and it is worth saying plainly.
# iceoryx2 exports every call, so its .sigs covers the whole ABI. NVENC does
# not: NvEncodeAPICreateInstance fills in an NV_ENCODE_API_FUNCTION_LIST, and
# every actual operation -- open session, create bitstream buffer, encode
# picture -- is a function pointer inside that struct. A .sigs file cannot
# express a struct layout, so the function list still needs a header that
# matches the driver's NVENCAPI_VERSION and is checked some other way.
#
# So this buys the loader and the start-time failure, not the whole ABI.
#
# Format: one C declaration per line. A comment starts with '#'.
#
# Signatures below are transcribed from the NVIDIA Video Codec SDK's
# nvEncodeAPI.h, which is NOT present on this desk. They are unverified against
# the header and must be checked before this file is used to generate anything.

NVENCSTATUS NvEncodeAPICreateInstance(NV_ENCODE_API_FUNCTION_LIST *functionList);
NVENCSTATUS NvEncodeAPIGetMaxSupportedVersion(uint32_t *version);
